weeks and  months  uh just to to give a few examples i  think  as i mentioned there will be the summit  in march on european defense  there will probably be news on the  strategic compass the big project on  european defense and security  french president macron and  mr schultz but also the other heads of  states and government from the european  member state will try to to find a  compromise and to bring  uh into reality what mr mccoy indeed  called  passing from uh  are giving europe uh a bigger stance in  international affairs  how about uh support domestically from  macron's agenda we know that he is  facing  domestic uh elections for example and he  set the the the bar at the eu level very  high  and saying i'm quote quoting here 2022  must be the year  of a european turning point  does he have the public support  i think he has the public support but as  we have seen  recently at the turn of the year uh this  public support is uh  could be prone  to to attacks and can shift um the  french put up or the french government i  should say put up a european flag  under the arc de triomphe a very  symbolic place uh on top of the tomb of  the unknown soldier in the center of  paris  and immediately you saw attacks on the  domestic scene from political opponents  predominantly from the political riot  the extreme right but also miss pikrez  from the moderate riot le repudica  so  i think macron mr macron knows that he  has a clear majority of french voters  who are pro-european and who want to see  progress  in european projects but  these these ideas or this majority will  constantly be under attack until the  elections in early april  jacob ross from the german council on  foreign relations thank you so much  thank you  well for more on that let's bring in  hartim shahada a researcher at the  german council on foreign relations here  in berlin uh macron has never made any  secret of his ambition to be the motor  for further european integration does  the eu presidency give him a welcome  platform to put his european record to  the forefront  well to be honest i think macau is now  the chance to really prove that he's  very pro-european that his ambitions  that he's already mentioned in 2017 um  at selburn university are am not very  far away from reality so now he has the  chance to really prove to germany as its  closest ally in europe and also to other  member states that he can really succeed  in what he has planned for for europe  well you mentioned germany that france  and germany are of course the two most  powerful nations in the eu and nothing  moves in the eu without at least one of  the two supporting it uh how are things  right now between paris and berlin  well i think with the new german  government there are some definitely  some um constraints now to the  relationship of france and germany so  with the discussion about eu taxonomy  there is  kind of a  yeah misunderstanding in between those  two countries so france on the one side  wants to push forward its nuclear agenda  whereas germany wants to um push forward  renewable energies so this is one of the  main points of discussion that we  observe at this point  is that a big dispute how much will it  strain relations  well i think right now is the time to  discuss things so macron is now the  chance to approach the other member  states especially germany and he now has  the chance to  convince other member states of his of  his ideas and um i'm not very sure if  germany is going to really accept his  ideas when it comes to nuclear energy so  germany will definitely oppose it  but  it is the chance to really find a  solution and a compromise and this is i  think  the the most basic idea of the rotating  eu presidency to find compromise and um  to gather the member states at one table  and to really discuss things  so macron has set the bar very high  saying 2022 must be the year of a  european turning point  how big is this support for this by the  french public you think  well  as i already mentioned macron has  put forward his ideas already in 2017  during his presidential um election  campaign  and it was perceived as very very  progressive by a lot of french citizens  so the french public has shown itself to  be very pro-european during this  campaign and the following years and i  would even go one step further and say  that the french do like the idea of  having considerable influence on an eu  level to of course push forward  certain political issues um that they  have interest in and even those who are  not very likely to support macron's  ideas of what they you should look like  in future want france to shine during  the next six months  even though they want they may not want  to change europe in  in a machinist way so i would say that  the majority of france will support the  idea of changing europe in either one  way or another  our team charter there from the german  council on foreign relations thank you  very much for your analysis  thank you
 

